WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.900
Does every wingbeat need its own nerve impulse?

2
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.940
A fly beats its wings so fast that the answer seemed in doubt.

3
00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:13.900
One recording put it to the test.

4
00:00:14.933 --> 00:00:16.833
The first film built one machine.

5
00:00:18.353 --> 00:00:21.273
Calcium switches it on, and myosin heads pull actin.

6
00:00:22.553 --> 00:00:25.893
Now the question is how a fly's flight muscle throws that switch.

7
00:00:27.017 --> 00:00:31.877
In vertebrate skeletal muscle, each contraction follows its own nerve impulse.

8
00:00:33.337 --> 00:00:36.157
So does the flight muscle of a locust or a dragonfly.

9
00:00:37.497 --> 00:00:39.037
This is called synchronous muscle.

10
00:00:40.067 --> 00:00:44.667
In nineteen forty-nine, John Pringle tested this rule in blue-bottle flies.

11
00:00:46.087 --> 00:00:49.167
He tethered each fly so that it beat its wings in place.

12
00:00:50.527 --> 00:00:53.227
Then he put a fine electrode into its flight muscle.

13
00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:59.527
If every beat needs its own impulse, the trace should show one large spike under

14
00:00:59.527 --> 00:01:02.567
every wingbeat. What do you think he saw?

15
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.000
The large spikes, which he linked to nerve impulses, came far less often than the

16
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.960
wings beat. And they were not timed to particular beats.

17
00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:20.520
The one-to-one rule failed.

18
00:01:21.583 --> 00:01:22.923
So what sets the beat?

19
00:01:24.503 --> 00:01:26.603
Picture the fly's thorax as a springy box.

20
00:01:27.883 --> 00:01:31.043
Two sets of power muscles pull on it in different directions.

21
00:01:32.217 --> 00:01:36.177
When one set shortens, the box bends, and the other set is stretched.

22
00:01:37.497 --> 00:01:39.497
These muscles are already switched on.

23
00:01:40.777 --> 00:01:43.697
In them, a stretch raises force, after a short delay.

24
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:47.520
So the stretched set builds force and shortens.

25
00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:50.380
That stretches the first set in turn.

26
00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:55.380
The muscles and the springy thorax keep the rhythm going between them.

27
00:01:56.433 --> 00:01:58.233
This is asynchronous muscle.

28
00:01:59.533 --> 00:02:04.233
It powers the wings of flies, bees and wasps, beetles and some other insects.

29
00:02:05.613 --> 00:02:10.513
In nineteen fifty-nine, Machin and Pringle reported stretch activation in beetle

30
00:02:10.513 --> 00:02:11.513
flight muscle.

31
00:02:12.233 --> 00:02:14.013
Does that make the nerve useless?

32
00:02:15.493 --> 00:02:21.433
No. Its few impulses keep the muscle's calcium raised, so it stays switched on.

33
00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:24.153
Without them, the wings stop.

34
00:02:25.167 --> 00:02:28.327
The pulling machine is the same as in the first film.

35
00:02:29.627 --> 00:02:30.907
What changed is the switch.

36
00:02:32.147 --> 00:02:34.807
The nerve holds it on, and stretch times each beat.

37
00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:38.640
Pringle's recordings left questions open.

38
00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:44.460
And how stretch raises force, at the level of the molecules, is still being worked

39
00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:45.460
out.

40
00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:47.990
So here is the rule for these insects.

41
00:02:47.990 --> 00:02:53.670
In asynchronous muscle, stretch times each beat, and the nerve keeps it on.
